From 1c66f980dfa07897315da88b6a04f17eb2fd3a6f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: abtraore <abdrahtraore97@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 21:18:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] glm5next: fused KDA gate epilogues + ssm_a NOSCAN
 placement

Two changes found while chasing decode launch overhead (4,484 nodes/token):

1. ggml: add GGML_OP_KDA_GATE (lower_bound * sigmoid(-((x + dt_b) * a)),
   per-head broadcast) and GGML_OP_KDA_OUT_GATE (rms_norm(x) * w *
   sigmoid(gate)), CUDA + CPU, replacing 9 elementwise nodes per KDA layer
   (~300 of 4,484 decode-graph nodes on GLM-5.3-Flash). Wired in glm5next
   behind cparams.fused_kda_gate with the standard probe and a
   LLAMA_FUSED_KDA_GATE_DISABLE escape hatch; the unfused chain stays as
   the fallback. Output is byte-identical fused vs unfused.

2. llama: create glm5next's ssm_a with LLM_TENSOR_SSM_A_NOSCAN, as
   qwen3next/qwen35 already do. The plain SSM_A placement probe tests
   GGML_OP_SSM_SCAN, which CUDA rejects for this shape, so all 34 ssm_a
   tensors silently landed in the CPU buffer ('cannot be used with
   preferred buffer type CUDA0'): a host->device copy per graph forever,
   and one scheduler split per KDA layer (11 -> 45 measured) for any
   fused op consuming the raw weight.

Measured on GLM-5.3-Flash UD-IQ1_S, 4x RTX 5090, 262,144 ctx, greedy:
spec-off decode 81.2/70.3 -> 83.1/72.8 tok/s (placement fix + fusion);
with MTP n-max 4 the gains dilute below noise (each graph eval already
amortizes ~5 tokens): 130-138 shallow / 57.8 at 85K depth, unchanged
within variance. The placement fix is the transferable part.
